# Aarogya Testing Rules

## General Requirement

A feature is not complete because its UI works.

A feature is complete when:

• Domain logic works
• State transitions work
• Error states work
• Offline behavior works
• Localization works
• Relevant tests pass
• Application builds successfully

## State Machine Tests

Test:

NORMAL
→ DISASTER_ALERT

DISASTER_ALERT
→ DISASTER_ACTIVE

DISASTER_ACTIVE
→ SURVIVAL

SURVIVAL
→ EMERGENCY_EVENT

EMERGENCY_EVENT
→ RESOLVED

Also test invalid transitions.

## Personal Baseline Tests

Test:

• Normal readings
• Small deviations
• Large deviations
• Missing readings
• Insufficient historical data
• Baseline initialization
• Baseline updates

Ensure that the system does not produce a risk result when insufficient data exists unless a documented fallback exists.

## Fall Detection Tests

Test:

• Realistic fall sequence
• Running
• Jumping
• Sitting quickly
• Dropping phone
• Sudden movement
• Fall followed by movement
• Fall followed by prolonged immobility

The goal is to reduce false positives in the prototype.

## Offline Tests

Disable internet and verify:

• App opens
• Normal health data remains accessible
• Local inference works
• Disaster guidance works
• Survival monitoring works
• Emergency event is created
• Emergency event is persisted
• Emergency packet remains queued

## Connectivity Recovery

Start offline.

Create emergency event.

Restore connectivity.

Verify:

Event is detected as pending.

Event enters queue.

Transmission occurs.

Delivery is confirmed.

Event is not duplicated.

## Communication Tests

Test:

Device A
→ Device B

Device B
→ Device C

Device C
→ connected node

Also test:

• Duplicate messages
• Expired messages
• Invalid messages
• Interrupted transmission
• Reconnection

## Localization Tests

Every user facing string must exist in:

English
Hindi

Test:

• Normal dashboard
• Disaster Mode
• Survival Mode
• Alerts
• Dialogs
• Error messages
• Empty states
• Permission messages

Test Hindi layout for overflow.

## UI Tests

Test:

• Small screens
• Large screens
• Dark/system configurations if supported
• Long Hindi text
• Offline state
• Loading state
• Error state
• Critical state

## Accessibility

Check:

• Contrast
• Font size
• Touch target size
• Screen reader labels where applicable
• Color-independent status communication

## Demo Tests

The complete SIH demonstration must work using the actual application pipeline.

Required sequence:

1. Normal Mode
2. Personal baseline
3. Flood trigger
4. Disaster Mode
5. Internet disabled
6. Offline guidance
7. Local communication
8. Survival Mode
9. Physiological deterioration simulation
10. Fall simulation
11. Emergency event creation
12. Local storage
13. Connectivity restoration
14. Emergency packet transmission
15. Rescue dashboard

## Simulation Integrity

A simulator may generate sensor data.

It must not bypass:

• Preprocessing
• AI inference
• Risk engine
• State machine
• Emergency logic

Bad:

simulateFall()
→ setEmergency(true)

Good:

simulateFall()
→ sensor stream
→ fall model
→ risk engine
→ emergency state

## Build Verification

Before considering a feature complete:

Run:

• Formatter
• Static analysis
• Unit tests
• Integration tests where applicable
• Application build

Do not leave known build errors unresolved.

## Regression

When changing shared components, test all affected modes.

A change to Normal Mode must not accidentally break:

• Disaster Mode
• Survival Mode
• Localization
• Emergency state
• Offline behavior

## Test Data

Use deterministic replay datasets for the primary demonstration.

Recommended:

normal.csv
flood_exertion.csv
health_deterioration.csv
fall.csv

These files are synthetic demonstration replay datasets. They are separate from the research datasets in DATASETS.md.

Keep demo data versioned and documented.

Do not use real personal health data in the repository.
